<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Banu Ka'b sheikhdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1722 CE – 1837 CE (approx.) · capital Fallahiyeh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Arab tribal sheikhdom of the Khuzestan marshes that built canals and a navy and challenged Persians, Ottomans and the East India Company.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kab">Banu Ka'b</a>, an Arab tribe from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, moved into the marshes of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karun-river">Karun</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a> in the late 17th and early 18th centuries, as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mushasha">Musha'sha</a> weakened. They settled at Qubban and later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shadegan">Fallahiyeh</a> (modern Shadegan), which became the seat of their sheikhs.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under Sheikh Salman ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> (c. 1737 to 1768) the sheikhdom reached its height. He dug canals and built dams for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and date cultivation, assembled a fleet of armed boats, and resisted campaigns by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire</a> and the Ottoman pasha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. In 1765 his ships seized vessels of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, provoking a British expedition. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kab">Ka'b</a> were Shia and Arabic-speaking.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Salman's death the sheikhdom fragmented. Its Muhaysin branch developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramshahr">Mohammerah</a>, and after an Ottoman sack of Mohammerah in 1837 leadership passed to what became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-mohammerah">Sheikhdom of Mohammerah</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1722 CE – 1837 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.97%;width:2.29%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1722 CE – 1837 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fallahiyeh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Salman ibn Sultan, Ghadban ibn Muhammad</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bani Ka'b, Ka'b of Dawraq</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1780&amp;focus=banu-kab-sheikhdom" aria-label="Banu Ka'b sheikhdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6089 1011 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Banu Ka'b sheikhdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6364 1269h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6409 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6409 1267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iran. <a href="/atlas?year=1780&amp;focus=banu-kab-sheikhdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
